WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Batch: half price for work nobody is waiting on.

2
00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:15.981
The Message Batches API takes a list of ordinary Messages requests, processes them asynchronously, and bills every token at 50 percent of the standard price, according to Anthropic’s pricing page.

3
00:00:16.261 --> 00:00:18.480
The discount stacks with caching discounts.

4
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:32.563
The docs give the operating envelope: up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB per batch, most batches complete within an hour, and anything not finished in 24 hours expires.

5
00:00:32.843 --> 00:00:35.296
Results stay downloadable for 29 days.

6
00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:45.240
Batch fits evaluation runs, backfills, nightly classification or enrichment, and report generation: anything where a person isn’t waiting on one item.

7
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:56.144
It doesn’t fit chat, agent loops (a batch request is single-shot, so there is no tool loop inside it), or fast mode and streaming, which the batch docs list as unsupported.

8
00:00:56.424 --> 00:00:58.856
Three engineering details catch teams out.

9
00:00:59.136 --> 00:01:03.936
Results arrive in any order, so key them by custom_id, never by position.

10
00:01:04.216 --> 00:01:14.264
Each result has its own status (succeeded, errored, canceled, expired), so a batch that “ended” can still contain failures you must retry or report.

11
00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:21.520
Caching inside a batch is best-effort: requests run concurrently, so not every request can read what another wrote.

12
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:29.373
Because batches can run longer than five minutes, the batch docs suggest the one-hour lifetime when many requests share a prefix.
